<DocsLayout
  title="Privacy & Data — Quakboard Docs"
  heading="Privacy & Data"
  summary="The technical detail of where your clipboard lives, what leaves the device, and the controls you have. For the plain-language version, see the privacy page."
>
  <h2>Where history is stored</h2>
  <p>
    Everything Quakboard captures goes into a single SQLite database on your
    machine:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>macOS</strong> — <code>~/Library/Application Support/quackboard/clipboard.db</code></li>
    <li><strong>Windows</strong> — <code>%APPDATA%\Quakboard\clipboard.db</code></li>
    <li><strong>Linux</strong> — <code>~/.local/share/quackboard/clipboard.db</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There's no hidden cache and no second copy. Delete that file and your
    history is gone. You can open it with any SQLite browser and read every row
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>What never leaves the device</h2>
  <p>
    With sync turned off — the default — <strong
      >nothing you copy ever leaves your computer</strong
    >. Quakboard makes no network requests for clipboard data, runs fully
    offline, and has no analytics or telemetry. The only outbound request the
    app can make is an optional check for updates, which sends just a version
    number.
  </p>

  <h2>What's encrypted when sync is on</h2>
  <p>
    If you <a href="/docs/sync">enable sync</a>, items are encrypted on the
    device before upload using a key derived from your passphrase. The relay
    stores ciphertext only. See the <a href="/docs/sync">Sync</a> page for the
    full model.
  </p>

  <h2>Incognito copy</h2>
  <p>
    Toggle <strong>Pause capture</strong> from the menu bar (or <code
      >⌘⇧.</code
    >) and Quakboard stops recording until you turn it back on. Anything you
    copy in that window is never written to the database. Use it for passwords,
    one-time codes, and anything you'd rather not keep.
  </p>

  <h2>Sensitive-content filtering</h2>
  <p>
    Quakboard automatically skips clipboard entries that apps mark as
    confidential — most password managers set this flag, so copied passwords are
    ignored by default. You can also add your own rules to exclude content
    matching a pattern, like anything that looks like a card number.
  </p>

  <h2>Retention and cleanup</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Cap history to a fixed number of items (100–5,000), oldest dropped first.</li>
    <li>Auto-delete anything older than 7, 30, 90, 180, or 365 days.</li>
    <li>Pinned snippets and favorites are <strong>never</strong> auto-deleted.</li>
    <li>
      <code>Settings → Data → Erase everything</code> wipes the database and, if
      enabled, your synced copies too.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Permissions we request</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Accessibility (macOS):</strong> only to send a paste keystroke into
      your active app. We don't read other apps.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notifications:</strong> optional, used for "copied" confirmations
      and sync status.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Global shortcuts:</strong> to open the window from anywhere.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
